1
00:00:00,229 --> 00:00:04,210
When we make a map, we are trying to convey
some information about a part of the real

2
00:00:04,210 --> 00:00:05,540
world.

3
00:00:05,540 --> 00:00:09,800
But every part of the world contains far more
detail than we could ever convey easily on

4
00:00:09,800 --> 00:00:14,290
a map, not least because maps always show
the world at a smaller scale than it really

5
00:00:14,290 --> 00:00:15,800
is.

6
00:00:15,800 --> 00:00:20,550
The infinite detail of the real world means
that it can sometimes be challenging to manage

7
00:00:20,550 --> 00:00:23,030
all the data we want to include on a map.

8
00:00:23,030 --> 00:00:27,590
In fact, to make any map we have to simplify
the real world.

9
00:00:27,590 --> 00:00:33,410
One way to do that is to think of the world
as being made up of different types of features.

10
00:00:33,410 --> 00:00:37,820
A feature could be something from the physical
world, such as the outline of a building,

11
00:00:37,820 --> 00:00:40,870
the course of a street, or the location of
a tree.

12
00:00:40,870 --> 00:00:45,181
A feature could also be a an event that happened
in a particular place, such as the location

13
00:00:45,181 --> 00:00:47,239
of an individual crime.

14
00:00:47,239 --> 00:00:52,829
There are infinite types of features that
we could put on maps, but computers typically

15
00:00:52,829 --> 00:00:59,930
store individual features in three different
ways: as points, as lines or as polygons.

16
00:00:59,930 --> 00:01:04,479
Points are used to represent physical features
such as street lights, as well as the locations

17
00:01:04,479 --> 00:01:06,580
of individual events.

18
00:01:06,580 --> 00:01:11,490
Lines are used to represent physical features
such as walls and roads, as well as behavioural

19
00:01:11,490 --> 00:01:15,590
features such as routes from one place to
another.

20
00:01:15,590 --> 00:01:19,710
Polygons are used to represent features like
the outline of a building or the area covered

21
00:01:19,710 --> 00:01:22,250
by a police force.

22
00:01:22,250 --> 00:01:28,460
Most files that store spatial information
can only store points, lines or polygons – not

23
00:01:28,460 --> 00:01:29,820
a mixture of different types.

24
00:01:29,820 --> 00:01:34,560
That means it’s usual for information about
different types of feature to be stored in

25
00:01:34,560 --> 00:01:35,560
different files.

26
00:01:35,560 --> 00:01:41,030
For example, if we wanted to find the nearest
police station to a set of crime locations,

27
00:01:41,030 --> 00:01:45,240
we would probably have a dataset of police
station locations and a separate dataset of

28
00:01:45,240 --> 00:01:47,509
crime locations.

29
00:01:47,509 --> 00:01:52,450
Once we have all the data we need, we can
build a map by adding each type of feature

30
00:01:52,450 --> 00:01:55,600
as a separate map layer.

31
00:01:55,600 --> 00:02:00,700
Every map is made up of multiple layers of
data, stacked on top of one another.

32
00:02:00,700 --> 00:02:05,119
For example, we might have a layer representing
roads, another representing buildings, and

33
00:02:05,119 --> 00:02:06,119
so on.

34
00:02:06,119 --> 00:02:10,220
It can be easy to end up trying to show a
lot of data on a map, either because the map

35
00:02:10,220 --> 00:02:14,060
shows a large area or because it shows a lot
of detail.

36
00:02:14,060 --> 00:02:18,060
Eventually the amount of data becomes hard
to work with.

37
00:02:18,060 --> 00:02:24,170
If that happens we can simplify the data by
converting the points, lines or polygons on

38
00:02:24,170 --> 00:02:27,190
the map into a raster layer.

39
00:02:27,190 --> 00:02:32,230
A raster layer is made up of a grid of cells
– sometimes called pixels – with each

40
00:02:32,230 --> 00:02:36,090
cell having a single value associated with
it.

41
00:02:36,090 --> 00:02:40,110
In this course we will see several examples
of raster layers on maps.

42
00:02:40,110 --> 00:02:44,330
The first are called base maps, which show
all the different physical features in an

43
00:02:44,330 --> 00:02:47,340
environment as a single layer.

44
00:02:47,340 --> 00:02:51,000
Base maps like this one can be very useful
because they mean we don’t have to deal

45
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:55,360
with separate layers for every type of feature
we want to show on a map.

46
00:02:55,360 --> 00:03:01,269
But because we can’t access each feature
individually, we cannot easily choose what

47
00:03:01,269 --> 00:03:05,840
information is shown on a base map, or how
features are represented.

48
00:03:05,840 --> 00:03:09,220
That means it’s important to choose a base
map that shows the information we need in

49
00:03:09,220 --> 00:03:10,760
a clear way.

50
00:03:10,760 --> 00:03:14,610
Fortunately, there are lots of useful base
maps available online.

51
00:03:14,610 --> 00:03:19,750
To summarise:
Spatial data can be represent as points, lines

52
00:03:19,750 --> 00:03:24,349
or polygons
We can build maps by stacking layers of features

53
00:03:24,349 --> 00:03:29,450
on top of each other
We can represent features as rasters to make

54
00:03:29,450 --> 00:03:30,610
them easier to work with